tt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ad90eb04705cb1be996bf4befa4b635ce029b62271cc0fbbf0bceb3e48284ffa","compact":"d7e8ef5cc410eac275d63121736e1dae6b7f18bc7c5a31ed6968c262b2dd997b","nasal":"daecb5d8ec64c92be6123ec3c7262d87a5156e2d4b04287e950f5f1875acd048"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":9,"english":"Then with heaps of dry wild wood covered with grass, they constructed a large raft. And the puissant Lakşmaņa tearing twigs from the ratan and the rose-apple, made a comfortable seat for Sita.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.841,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f710ca8dabfd4e867b2a2de139be2d10b285fbbc208da1e5fd4e8f8888c89478","compact":"2e0f48f03d00862cf7c91be208e4cffad74ae80a87a452cb606dc23505c254a2","nasal":"2e0f48f03d00862cf7c91be208e4cffad74ae80a87a452cb606dc23505c254a2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":10,"english":"Then Dasaratha's son, Rāma, made his bashful wife (in power) inconceivable like to Śrī herself, ascend the raft, and carefully laid beside Vaidehī her attires and ornaments as well as the hoe and the basket.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cdabf12f2fc21d8875b728fea7e4d1428b457876260eb36a847d0e06496a9423","compact":"6e50cb5eacca6c137e8b1b243d10e0d533b287e659516043f6085d58db074e88","nasal":"74e3b6364ba3c30f3d821cd63074743f356653d8f0a13e104e4ac155404af415"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":11,"english":"And by means of that raft they crossed that fleet-coursing daughter of the Sun, heaving with billows, the river Yamunā with her banks abounding with innumerable trees.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.676,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a4df05b26cc0a1573a9f7b889859dea75dd8844de66e0b26b3aea2a57d1cb1b5","compact":"bb31047df7bf877019e8fda4526ef511be975c90b0ab6b222fa2cca6e288220a","nasal":"bb31047df7bf877019e8fda4526ef511be975c90b0ab6b222fa2cca6e288220a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":12,"english":"Then renouncing the raft, and passing by the woods adjoining the Yamunā, they came upon a banian Syāma by name, of cool shade and verdant foliage.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.87,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"160120d33b2e5eae03f01f9d440f4dd28223f448d75e1eb596245cbc63f90c5c","compact":"6c7fe36b6d93e66942d6456a4cb286803b0b1e45eeaebc3b5111d4058c443148","nasal":"6c7fe36b6d93e66942d6456a4cb286803b0b1e45eeaebc3b5111d4058c443148"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":13,"english":"On coming to the banian, Vaidehī saluted it saying, “O mighty tree, I bow to you. May my husband fulfil his vow; and may we behold Kausalyā and the illustrious Sumitrā.” Having thus prayed with joined hands, the intelligent Sītā went away.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.687,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"305fcecd6e3efc7e3bd2beee01e4441fee21fd74e888efc28c64e1ae78d1f6b0","compact":"83543243554e90bfc1c2cdc33237710ef169f3a1d04ac755ae06a82cff7c0458","nasal":"9579873e4d4bd83e6e1d1c3e36d2331bc31f78748560c630a371f91f171b8674"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":14,"english":"And having proceeded just a Krosa, those brothers, Rāma and Lakşmaņa, having killed many a sacred deer, began to range in the woods of the Yarnunā.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.884,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"377185474bb353ca788f0ac0e9c86255806433c6d11a2c577df203ec8db3fd2c","compact":"06b21f258ee3f46320cb7f5a30fb478085338e3db89cdd393be8331400a249fa","nasal":"06b21f258ee3f46320cb7f5a30fb478085338e3db89cdd393be8331400a249fa"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-49-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":49,"verse":15,"english":"And having disported in the beautiful woods resounding with multitudes of peacocks and inhabited by elephants and monkeys, they looking as lively as ever, coming to the level banks of the river, took up their quarters there.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-55-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2049015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6b2bf64a0105de64c033e2e62aad8c7043cb2f7d4da797373850b597782e3838","compact":"09b05a25e93e1a20870b8c1bf08d4e114ce798a877854436a5d24e1f2ece661c","nasal":"09b05a25e93e1a20870b8c1bf08d4e114ce798a877854436a5d24e1f2ece661c"}}]
